[TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Campus DNS service on srv1 (BIND/named) — its recursion policy. Mechanism: PC1’s resolver points to 10.5.0.4 (srv1). For any external domain (e.g., example.com), srv1 refuses the query instead of resolving it upstream. Applications then retry, fall back to alternate resolvers (e.g., DoH) or different transports, causing long delays and intermittent “hangs” before pages finally load. Localization: DNS server srv1 at 10.5.0.4. Evidence: PC1 resolv.conf uses nameserver 10.5.0.4. A direct test from PC1 shows “server can’t find example.com: REFUSED” against 10.5.0.4, while an internal zone works (“jeremysitlab.com → 10.5.0.4”). On srv1, BIND is listening on 10.5.0.4:53 and configured with “recursion no;”
[FINAL] stop=no-more-tool-calls tool_calls=41
[SUBMITTED] parsed_ok=True is_anomaly=True faulty_devices=['srv1']
[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Campus DNS service on srv1 (BIND/named) — its recursion policy. Mechanism: PC1’s resolver points to 10.5.0.4 (srv1). For any external domain (e.g., example.com), srv1 refuses the query instead of resolving it upstream. Applications then retry, fall back to alternate resolvers (e.g., DoH) or different transports, causing long delays and intermittent “hangs” before pages finally load. Localization: DNS server srv1 at 10.5.0.4. Evidence: PC1 resolv.conf uses nameserver 10.5.0.4. A direct test from PC1 shows “server can’t find example.com: REFUSED” against 10.5.0.4, while an internal zone works (“jeremysitlab.com → 10.5.0.4”). On srv1, BIND is listening on 10.5.0.4:53 and configured with “recursion no;” in /etc/bind/named.conf.options, confirming it is authoritative-only and will not resolve internet domains. The network path itself is healthy: PC1 pings default gateway 10.1.0.1 sub‑ms and reaches 10.5.0.4 in ~2 ms; no interface errors or congestion observed on PC1 or aswa2. Root cause: srv1’s BIND is intentionally configured as non-recursive (authoritative only), but client PCs are using it as their sole DNS resolver for internet lookups. Corrective fix: Enable recursion and set appropriate forwarders on srv1 (or provide root-hint recursion), or reconfigure clients to use a recursive DNS (e.g., ISP/central resolver). Example on srv1: set “recursion yes;” and add forwarders { <ISP DNS IPs>; }; then reload named. This will eliminate DNS refusals and the perceived browsing lag. 
=== END ===